   const logReadOperation = async (collectionName, docId) => {
     const docRef = db.collection(collectionName).doc(docId);
     const doc = await docRef.get();
     if (doc.exists()) {
       const readMetric = {
         name: 'readCount',
         value: 1
       };
       await admin.firestore().collection('metrics').doc('readCount').set(readMetric);
     }
   };
   ```

4. **Analyze the Data**: Once you have logged the read operations, you can go to the Firebase console and view the performance metrics under the "Insights" tab. You can filter the metrics by the custom events you logged to see the read counts.

5. **Use Firebase Analytics**: Additionally, you can use Firebase Analytics to log custom events related to your reads and writes. This can provide more detailed insights into user behavior and application usage.

By following these steps, you can better understand the read patterns in your Firebase application and identify any potential issues or inefficiencies.
